    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_aad8419373265375ae351ad79c3b5218c59bb68b7f487566c74e15a24fb5f7c9",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Across the easy half-plane, the interior strip, and the critical line, the source writes a full-interval moment as a main term plus a remainder; subtracting endpoint formulas makes the main term proportional to H and leaves the remainder difference, so an asymptotic on a short interval is available only when H dominates the relevant error scale. Absolute convergence gives an O(1) error for fixed sigma>1, moment-threshold or divisor-error estimates give sigma- and k-dependent powers in the strip, and oscillatory or spectral control supplies the recorded critical-line scales for the second and fourth moments.",
